# Shared team review rooms

## Architecture
- `migrations/0002_teams.sql` (append-only): `teams`, `team_members` (role `owner`/`member`), `team_uploads`.
- `src/teams.ts`: Hono sub-app mounted at `/api/teams` behind the host's `requireAuth` and its same-origin mutation guard. Every request reads membership from D1 (no authorization cache), so removal and logout take effect on the next request or handshake.
  - Teams and membership: owner-only management; unknown user 400, owner target 409, non-owner 403. Non-members get 403 on team resources.
  - Uploads: raw bytes up to 1 MiB, bounded from the actual (chunked) stream, 413 when exceeded. R2 key is `teams/<verified team>/<server UUID>`; lookups always filter by `team_id`, so another team's ID yields 404 and deletes nothing. Personal `/api/uploads` is untouched.
  - Room gateway `GET /:team/rooms/:room/ws`: requires an exact same-origin `Origin`, no query string, room `[A-Za-z0-9_-]{1,40}`, current membership. It forwards to the DO with freshly built headers only (verified user and a lease), so client identity headers are never passed on. The DO namespace is used only here.
- `src/room.ts`: `TeamRoom`, one SQLite Durable Object per `[team, room]`. It uses `acceptWebSocket`, attachments (`userId`, lease expiry) and ping/pong auto-response, with no socket map or timer. A message is inserted (AUTOINCREMENT `seq`, so it keeps rising after pruning) before it is broadcast to every socket. 200 rows are retained and the snapshot is the latest 50. There is no rate limit.
- The lease is `min(4 min, session expiry)`. After it passes, a socket's next message closes it, and a client must reconnect, which re-checks D1.
- `public/teams.html`: the UI described in the spec (`data-testid` controls). Status values are `idle`, `connecting`, `connected to <room>`, `closed…` and `error…`; "disconnected" is avoided on purpose so that it never matches `connected`. Text is rendered with `textContent`. Reconnecting detaches and closes the previous socket first.

## Follow-up: viewers, live authority and retry-safe messages
- `migrations/0003_viewer_role.sql` (additive): SQLite cannot alter a CHECK, so `team_members` is rebuilt with roles `owner`/`member`/`viewer`, copying every existing row. 0000–0002 are untouched, and no Durable Object migration tag was added or changed.
- Roles: `PUT …/members/:userId` accepts `member` or `viewer` (anything else is 400); the owner stays immutable (409). Viewers can list teams (the real role is returned), connect, read history and live messages, and download team files. Upload POST/DELETE return 403 for them. Reads need any membership; writes need `owner` or `member`.
- Live authority in `TeamRoom`: the gateway passes the verified session ID and team ID in its freshly built headers, and they are stored in the socket attachment. For each send the DO re-reads session (not revoked, not expired) and membership from D1 before any validation, deduplication or ack. A removed or logged-out sender gets `{type:"error",code:"forbidden"}` and the socket is closed. A viewer gets the same error and stays connected. Before each broadcast every recipient is re-authorized in one D1 batch. Unauthorized recipients are skipped and closed. The 4-minute lease still applies on top of this. A socket opened before this change has no session ID in its attachment and is closed on its next message.
- Ordering: sends and handshakes run through an in-memory promise chain (ordering only, no authoritative state). Commits are therefore serialized, deliveries leave in `seq` order, and a new socket's snapshot never overlaps with a live event.
- `clientId` (`[A-Za-z0-9_-]{1,80}`, optional): the `messages` table gets a nullable `clientId` column (added on demand to rooms that already have history; old rows read as before). A separate `dedupe(userId, clientId, seq, text)` table is written in the same synchronous transaction as the message and is never pruned, so retries stay idempotent beyond the latest-50 window and across restarts. An exact retry gets `{type:"ack",seq,clientId}` on the retrying socket only. The same ID with different text gets `{type:"error",code:"conflict",clientId}`. An invalid ID gets `code:"invalid"`. IDs are scoped by user and by room. The first send of a new message does not produce an ack; the sender sees the normal `message` event, which includes `clientId`.
- UI: the page records each team's role from `GET /api/teams`. For a viewer `message-send` and `team-upload` are disabled, while connect and reading still work. A role change shows after a refresh, but the server enforces it immediately. The page never resends anything after reconnecting.

## Commands
`pnpm typecheck`, `pnpm test`, `pnpm build` (dry run), `pnpm db:local`. Tests are in `test/teams.test.ts` (API, uploads, handshake guards, broadcast, pruning and validation) and `test/viewers.test.ts` (viewer permissions, downgrade, removal and logout on open sockets, re-adding, `clientId` retries, conflicts, simultaneous retries, dedupe after pruning, and no replay for revoked or read-only senders); the original tests are unchanged.

## Limitations
- Membership management is API-only. Only the two demo accounts can be added.
- Removal, downgrade and logout apply to application sends made after the HTTP response completes. Sockets are closed lazily, on the next send or delivery attempt, not at once. A socket that was removed and then re-added before any traffic was sent would keep its old connection. Messages already queued before the change are not retracted. Delivery is at-most-once with no replay, and only the snapshot recovers history. No billing or eviction-timing claims.
- Deduplication records grow without bound (one small row per distinct `clientId` per room); there is no expiry. Each send costs two D1 queries (sender, then recipients).
- Uploads are buffered in memory (1 MiB) and the team file list is not persisted or listable, since there is no list endpoint. R2 and D1 writes are not atomic: a failure between them can leave an orphan object. No quotas.
- Room text length is counted in UTF-16 code units. The 100-connection cap per room is a feature limit, not abuse prevention. Browser behaviour is not covered by automated browser tests.
- Room history survives restarts through DO SQLite persistence, but it was not verified by an automated Wrangler restart test.
